+# Invalidated owner-adopted attempt recovery implementation plan
+
+**Goal:** Add the requested incident and v2 migration recovery while preserving every v1 contract.
+
+**Architecture:** Keep v1 validation/capture intact; dispatch v2 to additive lifecycle and incident validators and reuse safe archival/restoration operations. Read relocated evidence through explicit byte overlays, retaining real Git checks. No runtime or workflow behavior changes.
+
+**Scope:** retirement source bindings, broad evidence, materialization, attempt migration, lazy exports, temporary-Git behavioral tests. No dependencies, workflow changes, or repository-specific policy.
+
+**Tradeoff:** Separate schema validation duplicates some v1 shape checks but avoids changing its historical contracts.
+
+- [x] Derive strict first-parent predecessor lineage with raw message and path projections; test temporary Git ranges and malformed trailers.
+- [x] Extend materialization and broad-evidence readers and review validators with byte overlays and immutable committed fallback; preserve existing issue codes and test lineage/reviews.
+- [x] Build and validate incidents from bound evidence and derived lifecycle facts.
+- [x] Add v2 capture, strict schema dispatch, logical archive view, apply/postvalidate replay and CLI.
+- [x] Collect new tests, run narrow retirement checks, then broad retirement suite with 16 pytest workers in tmux. Review diff and verification evidence.
+
+The user supplied the complete schema/API design and authorizes implementation. Follow it as the governing additive contract; no design approval or worktree creation is needed. Execution uses subagents for the independent lineage and validator-reader changes, with integration and v2 implementation in the parent workspace.
+
+Verification: 850 retirement tests passed with `TMPDIR=/tmp pytest -q -n 16 --dist=worksteal` across the migration, incident, broad evidence, source binding, lineage, and overlay modules. New tests collected successfully; `git diff --check` passed. Real temporary-Git CLI capture/apply/postvalidate checks cover the end-to-end evidence surface. Runtime/workflow smoke checks are unnecessary because no runtime, workflow, DSL, or provider code changed. The full repository suite was not run.
